[THOUGHT] The COMMON-SYMPTOM layer is now complete: acute mechanical radicular low back pain without red-flag systemic or neurological features. Next, I must pivot to the DISCRIMINATING layer — where objective data will confirm or refute the leading diagnosis (e.g., lumbar disc herniation) and exclude alternatives. Key domains remaining: past medical/surgical history (e.g., prior spine surgery, cancer, osteoporosis), medications/allergies (e.g., anticoagulants that could complicate imaging or intervention), social/exposure history (e.g., IV drug use → risk of spinal infection), targeted physical exam (e.g., straight leg raise, motor/sensory/Reflex testing), and definitive testing (e.g., MRI if indicated). Since physical exam findings can rapidly confirm radiculopathy (e.g., positive SLR, focal weakness), it’s the most efficient next step — non-invasive, immediate, and highly discriminatory.

[TEST] Perform a focused lumbar spine physical examination, including:  
- Straight leg raise test (supine, passive elevation of extended leg) on both sides;  
- Assessment of motor strength in ankle dorsiflexion (L4–L5), great toe extension (L5), and plantar flexion (S1);  
- Sensory testing over the lateral thigh (L2–L3), lateral calf/foot (L4–L5), and sole of foot (S1);  
- Patellar and Achilles deep tendon reflexes.

